<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>
  <section xml:id="two-d-games-space-farm-06-sound_effects">
    <title>Sound Effects: Adding Atmosphere</title>
    <introduction>
    <p>
        The reality that space is quiet because of a vacuum doesn't 
        change the fact that most people benefit from audio feedback.
        In addition there is sound in the space ship that a person is
        traveling in. So, it's reasonable to consider all of the sound 
        that is produced in the game from either sound generated by the 
        ship or the sound of the ship making contact with some other 
        object. This section will discuss background music and a 
        game over tune and then sound effects based on the interaction
        with the environment. The <url href="https://wcu-cs-cooperlab.github.io/Fall2026GameDev/book-assets/space-farm/sound.zip">sounds link</url>
        has a zip of the sound files. Download these, unzip the file, and add the sound directory to your project.
    </p>
    </introduction>
    <subsection xml:id="two-d-games-space-farm-sound-effects-music">
      <title>Background Music</title>
      <p> 
        The background music is a sample from <url href="https://opengameart.org/content/besai-crystal-gardens-zone-11">Besai Crystal Gardens Zone 11</url>. 
        The file is located in <c>res://sound/music/BesaiCrystalGardensRemix.ogg</c>. In your main scene, add an
        AudioStreamPlayer Node and rename it <c>MusicStream</c>. Then, in the FileSystem Dock, select
        the <c>res://sound/music/BesaiCrystalGardensRemix.ogg</c> file and then open the Import Dock
        to set the Loop to On, and the Loop Offset to 9.0 as shown in 
        <xref ref="space-farm-background-music-import-dock" />. This makes it so that the music will start 9 seconds in, 
        where the music is a little bit louder so that the music is noticed right away. 
        You will also probably want to adjust the volume of the music down so that it mixes well with 
        the other sound effects discussed below. You can do this by setting the Volume dB in the 
        MusicStream's Inspector. Set the value to be negative 
        if you want it quieter, and positive if you want it louder. The example version uses
        about -30 for the Volume dB setting as shown in 
        <xref ref="space-farm-background-music-inspector-dock" />.
      </p>
      <p>
        In your main script, add <c>$MusicStream.play()</c> at the end of your <c>start_game()</c>
        method, and add <c>$MusicStream.stop()</c> at the end of your <c>game_over()</c>
        method.
      </p>
      <p> 
        When the game ends, we want to have a short ending music play. To do that, make another
        AudioStreamPlayer, and rename it to GameOverPlayer. Add <c>res://sound/music/gameover.ogg</c>
        as the Stream in the inspector as shown in 
        <xref ref="space-farm-gameover-music-inspector-dock" />.
      </p>
      <p>
         In your main script, add <c>$GameOverPlayer.play()</c> at the end of your <c>game_over()</c>
        method.
      </p>
      <p>
        Run the main scene to verify that the music plays when you start the game, stops when
        the game is over, and the game over sound happens at the end of the game.
      </p>
      <figure>
        <caption>Background Music</caption>
        <sidebyside>
    <figure xml:id="space-farm-background-music-import-dock">
      <caption>Background Music Import</caption>
      <image source="space-farm-background-music-loop.webp" width="75%" >
         <shortdescription>The Import Dock is shown with Loop On and 9.0 as the loop offset.</shortdescription>
     </image>
      </figure>
      <figure xml:id="space-farm-background-music-inspector-dock">
      <caption>Background Music Inspector</caption>
      <image source="space-farm-background-music-inspector.webp" width="75%" >
         <shortdescription>The inspector is shown with the volume db set to -30.</shortdescription>
     </image>
      </figure>
      <figure xml:id="space-farm-gameover-music-inspector-dock"> 
      <caption>Game Over Music Inspector</caption>
      <image source="space-farm-gameover-music-inspector.webp" width="75%" >
         <shortdescription>The inspector is shown with gameover.ogg as the Stream.</shortdescription>
     </image>
      </figure>
    </sidebyside>
</figure>
      </subsection>
      <subsection xml:id="two-d-games-space-farm-sound-effects-crystal">
      <title>Crystal Effects</title>
      <p>
        As the crystal changes form, we add a sound effect to indicate each change. Open the 
        prize scene and add an AudioStreamPlayer and rename it <c>GrowthPlayer</c>.
        In the Inspector, set the Volume dB to about -40 dB. Then add the following code to your
        prize.gd script.
      </p>
      <p>
        First make two member variables for the streams we'll be using.
      </p>
      <program language="gdscript">
var growOne:AudioStream = load("res://sound/effects/prize_grow1.ogg")
var growTwo:AudioStream = load("res://sound/effects/prize_grow2.ogg")
      </program>
      <p>
        Then, make a new update mathod that will handle both the texture changes and
        optionally play sound effects based on whether the growth stage has changed.
      </p>
      <program language="gdscript">
func update(texture:Texture2D,value:int):
    if $Sprite2D.texture != texture or self.value != value:
        $Sprite2D.texture = texture
        self.value = value
        if texture == med:
            $GrowthPlayer.stream = growOne
            $GrowthPlayer.play()
        elif texture == large:
            $GrowthPlayer.stream = growTwo
            $GrowthPlayer.play()
      </program>
      <p>
        After adding the <c>update()</c> method, replace each place in the code in <c>_process()</c>
        that set the $Sprite2D.texture and value, to instead call the <c>update()</c> function
        with the corresponding texture and value.
      </p>
      <p>
        Run the prize scene to verify that the sounds are happening when the crystals change form.
      </p>
      </subsection>
      <subsection xml:id="two-d-games-space-farm-sound-effects-player">
      <title>Player Effects</title>
        <introduction>
      <p>
        The player sounds will involve using the thrusters, everything involved with the lasso,
        and damage to the ship.
      </p>
      <p>
        For the lasso, we want a sound when launching the lasso and then 2 different sounds based on whether
        the lasso caught the crystal or missed. Since, the lasso isn't making it's own sounds, we'll use
        signals to let the player know when the state of the lasso has changed. Then the player will add 
        the necessary sound effects.
      </p>
      <p>
        For the thrusters we'll just need one sound, and for damage, we'll have a different sound
        based on how big the crystal is that we hit.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <subsubsection xml:id="two-d-games-space-farm-sound-effects-player-collection">
        <title>Lasso Effects</title>
        <p>
            The Lasso effects will be initiated by events with the lasso, and a signal will be sent to 
            the Player who will ultimately play the corresponding sound.
        </p>
<paragraphs>
        <title>Collector Scene</title>
      
<p>
    In order for the audio to play from the player based on collector events, the Collector will use a signal
    called <c>collect_state(value: CollectState)</c> and an enum called <c>CollectState</c> that
    enumerates the different states the collector may be in.
</p>
 <program language="gdscript">
enum CollectState {LAUNCHED, MISS, HIT_SMALL, HIT_MED, HIT_LARGE}

signal collect_state(value: CollectState)
</program>
<p>
    By sending a signal, we make sure that the sound will play even after the Node that caused the signal
    has been freed. For instance, when the collector misses, it will reach its maximum size, 
    so we listen for the max size reached
    signal and then send our own signal that the collector missed, since if we play a sound as we are
    being freed, most of the sound will not play.
    So, we start by connecting the GrowingArea max_size_reached
    signal to the scene and using the default method name <c>_on_max_size_reached(source: GrowingArea)</c>
    Then add the following code:
</p>
 <program language="gdscript">
func _on_max_size_reached(source: GrowingArea) -> void:
    emit_signal("collect_state",CollectState.MISS)
</program>
<p>
    When the collector hits, a signal is already sent to main's collect method, which in turn
    calls the playser's collect method. So, when the player's collect method is called,
    we can play the hit sound, so we don't need an additional collect state to play a sound 
    for our current interaction. (When we do more complicated interactions, this will change.)
</p>
<p>
    Similarly, the player already has a <c>launch()</c> method, so the only signal we need so far is the 
    one carrying a MISS CollectState.
</p>
</paragraphs>
      <paragraphs>
        <title>Player Scene</title>
      
      <p> Open the player scene and
        add an 
        AudioStreamPlayer and rename it to <c>SoundEffect</c>. Then edit the player.gd
        script with the following.
      </p>
      <p>
        First, add an AudioStream for the launch, miss, and hit sound files. 
      </p>
       <program language="gdscript">
var collectorHitStream: AudioStream = load("res://sound/effects/collector_hit_softest.ogg")
var collectorLaunchedStream: AudioStream = load("res://sound/effects/collector_launch_softer.ogg")
var collectorMissedStream: AudioStream = load("res://sound/effects/collector_miss_softer.ogg")
       </program>
       <p>
        Next, add the following code to the <c>launch()</c>  method, right before calling
        <c>collector.start($Launcher.global_transform)</c>.(Make sure it's indented correctly)
      </p>
       <program language="gdscript">
$SoundEffect.stream = collectorLaunchedStream
$SoundEffect.play()      
       </program>
       <p>
        Next, in the <c>collect()</c>  method, add the following. (Make sure it's indented correctly)
      </p>
       <program language="gdscript">
$SoundEffect.stream = collectorHitStream
$SoundEffect.play()   
       </program> 
<p>
        Now, for the miss event we need to connect the signal from each collector, then when a MISS state is 
        received, the miss sound should play. First, in the <c>launch()</c>  method, add the following
        right before calling
        <c>collector.start($Launcher.global_transform)</c>.(Make sure it's indented correctly)
</p>
       <program language="gdscript">
collector.collect_state.connect(receive_collect_state)
</program>
<p>
    Then we need to write the <c>receive_collect_state</c> method to play the miss sound when
    the MISS state is received:
</p>
<program language="gdscript">
func receive_collect_state(coll_state: Collector.CollectState) -> void:
    if coll_state == Collector.CollectState.MISS:
        $SoundEffect.stream = collectorMissedStream
        $SoundEffect.play()
</program>
</paragraphs>
<paragraphs>
    <title>Sound Testing</title>
    <p>
        Open the player scene and run it. When you launch the lasso, you should hear the launch sound
        and when the lasso disappears, you should here the miss sound. 
    </p>
    <p> 
        Open the main scene and run it. Now, when you collect a prize, you should here the hit sound.
    </p>
</paragraphs>
</subsubsection>
<subsubsection xml:id="two-d-games-space-farm-sound-effects-player-thruster">
        <title>Thruster Sound Effects</title>
        <p>
            The thruster effects will happen as player is propelled forward.
        </p>
<paragraphs>
        <title>Player Scene</title>
       <p> 
        Open the player scene and add a new AudioStreamPlayer and rename it to ThrustEffect. Then add the 
        rocket_smooth.ogg file as the Stream in the Inspector. Then make
        a method called <c>thrusting(val: bool)</c> that either starts or stops the thrust, the particle stream,
        and the sound effect:
    </p>
<program language="gdscript">  
func thrusting(val: bool) -> void:
    if val:
        thrust = transform.x * thrust_power
        $Rocket.emitting = true
        if not $ThrustEffect.playing:
            $ThrustEffect.play()
    else:
        thrust = Vector2.ZERO
        $Rocket.emitting = false
        $ThrustEffect.stop()
</program>
<p>
    Next, modify the <c>get_input()</c> method by removing the <c>if/else</c> clause 
    related to the thrust input (<c>Input.is_action_pressed("thrust")</c>) and replacing 
    it with the following (making sure that the indentation is correct):
</p>
<program language="gdscript">
var thrustAction: bool = Input.is_action_pressed("thrust")
thrusting(thrustAction)
</program>
<p>
    Finally, we want to make sure that when the ship is wrecked the rocket sound stops.
    So, set <c>thrusting(false)</c> in the change_state function when the State is WRECKED:
</p>
<program language="gdscript">
func change_state(update : State):
    match update:
        # ... leave other code above
        State.WRECKED:
            thrusting(false)
            $CollisionPolygon2D.set_deferred("disabled",true)
        # ... leave other code below
</program>

</paragraphs>
</subsubsection>

      <subsubsection xml:id="two-d-games-space-farm-sound-effects-player-damage">
      <title>Damage Effects</title>
      <p>
        When the ship bumps into a crystal, it takes damage, so we want to make a sound for that.
        So, open the player scene and add an AudioStreamPlayer and rename it DamageEffect.
      </p>
      <p>
        Since there are 3 different stages of size for the crystal, we'll have 3 different
        sounds for each amount of damage. We'll load these as streams in the player.gd script.
      </p>
      <program language="gdscript">
var damageSmallStream: AudioStream = load("res://sound/effects/crash_small.ogg")
var damageMedStream: AudioStream = load("res://sound/effects/crash_med.ogg")
var damageLargeStream: AudioStream = load("res://sound/effects/crash_large.ogg")
      </program>
      <p>
      Then, since we'll be using the damage values in more than one place we'll name the damage 
      amounts in the prize.gd file as static variables.    
    </p>
      <program language="gdscript">
static var smallValue: int = 10
static var medValue: int = 25
static var largeValue: int = 50
      </program>
      <p>
      Then, (still in prize.gd) in the <c>_process()</c> method change 10 to <c>smallValue</c>, 25 to <c>medValue</c>, and 50
      to <c>largeValue</c>.    
    </p>
    <p> Then go back to the player.gd script, create a  <c>hit_object</c> method to 
        call <c>play_damage()</c>, which will play different sounds based on the value, 
        followd by <c>take_damage()</c>:
    </p>
     <program language="gdscript">
func hit_object(damage: int) -> void:
	play_damage(damage)
	take_damage(damage)
      </program>
    <p>
         Then,
        add the play_damage method as shown below:
    </p>
      <program language="gdscript">
func play_damage(value: int) -> void:
    match value:
        Prize.smallValue:
            $DamageEffect.stream = damageSmallStream
            $DamageEffect.play()
        Prize.medValue:
            $DamageEffect.stream = damageMedStream
            $DamageEffect.play()
        Prize.largeValue:
            $DamageEffect.stream = damageLargeStream
            $DamageEffect.play()
      </program>
 <p>
         In the main.gd file, change the connection from <c>prize.hit.connect($Player.take_damage)</c>
         to <c>prize.hit.connect($Player.hit_object)</c>
    </p>
    <p>Play the main scene to make sure there are 3 distinct sounds when the ship hits different sized crystals.
    </p>
      </subsubsection>
      </subsection>
      <subsection xml:id="two-d-games-space-farm-sound-effects-play-testing">
      <title>Play Testing</title>
      <p>
        Now, run the main scene, and listen to the sounds and music. 
        Change the volume of each AudioStreamPlayer so that no sound 
        is overpowering another.
      </p>
      <p>Once you are finished play testing, go to the next 
        section to add the heads up display.
      </p>
      </subsection>
    </section>
